course: dictionary
lesson: 437
title: Digging up the past
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Archaeology is the study of the human past. Archaeologists dig up old things that people left behind, such as tools, bones, pots, coins and buildings. Then they examine these carefully to learn how people lived long ago.
explain: Archaeologists dig carefully, in layers. Deeper layers are usually older, so they can tell us about older times. They record everything they find, so no clue is lost.
explain: «Archaeology tells a plainer story: villages on seven hills by the river Tiber grew together into a town.» Archaeology shows what really happened, using things people left behind. It does not just repeat legends.
explain: Historians often check old stories against archaeology. «The Primary Chronicle... mixes facts and legends, so historians check it against archaeology and foreign sources.» When a legend and the evidence do not match, the evidence matters a lot.
fact: Archaeology is the study of the human past by digging up and examining old things.
fact: An archaeological dig is when archaeologists dig up a place carefully to find clues about the past.
fact: Archaeology helps us learn about times before writing, such as the Stone Age and the Romans.
fact: Archaeologists dig in layers, and deeper layers are usually older.
fact: «Legend says Rome was founded in 753 BC by the twins Romulus and Remus», but archaeology shows Rome grew from small villages on hills by the river Tiber.
exercise: What is archaeology? | Learning to paint pictures of animals | *The study of the human past by digging up and examining old things | Writing new stories about the future | The study of stars and planets
exercise: «Archaeology tells a plainer story.» What does plainer mean here? | Written in a secret code | Longer and more exciting | *Simpler and more true, without legends | Painted in bright colours
exercise: Archaeologists dig in layers. Which layer is usually the oldest? | The layer with the newest things | The top layer | The layer nearest the surface | *The deepest layer
test: Which of these would an archaeologist dig up? | Sweets and crisps | *Bones, pots and old coins | Video games | Newspapers from this week
test: Why do historians check a legend against archaeology? | To make the legend longer | To prove the legend is wrong without looking at anything | To hide the facts | *To see whether the evidence supports the story
exam: Why is archaeology important for learning about times before writing? | *Because the things people left behind show how they lived | Because old people always remember everything | Because writing is always wrong | Because it is only about stories and legends
